{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 13, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "וכל קרבן מנחתך במלח תמלח", :translit "u'chol korban minchat'cha b'melach timlach", :gloss "and every offering of your meal-offering with salt you shall salt"} {:he "מלח", :translit "melach", :gloss "salt"} {:he "בלשכת המלח", :translit "b'lishkat ha-melach", :gloss "in the Chamber of Salt"} {:he "על הכבש", :translit "al ha-kovesh", :gloss "on the ramp"} {:he "על המזבח", :translit "al ha-mizbeach", :gloss "on the altar"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["מלח"]}], :frame {:text "The salt used in the Temple service was stored in a designated area called the Chamber of Salt, referenced among the chambers of the Temple in tractate Middot. The verse Leviticus 2:13 mandates salting all offerings, and the phrase 'a covenant of salt' (Numbers 18:19) links salt to the eternal priestly covenant. The three-fold mention of salt in this verse is read as corresponding to three physical locations where salt figures in the sacrificial procedure.", :sources ["Leviticus 2:13" "Numbers 18:19" "Middot 5:3"]}} {:quotes [{:he "תקריב מלח", :translit "takriv melach", :gloss "you shall offer salt"} {:he "זה יביא מלח משל ציבור", :translit "zeh yavi melach mi'shel tzibbur", :gloss "this one brings salt from the public's [funds]"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :gematria, :a "תקריב מלח", :b "זה יביא מלח משל ציבור"}], :frame {:text "Temple sacrifices were funded from two sources: communal public funds and individual private contributions. The public treasury (terumat ha-lishkah) was used for the daily communal offerings and their accompanying meal-offerings and libations, as described in tractate Shekalim. Since the meal-offering of Leviticus 2:13 requires salt, the gematria equation signals that the salt for this offering is supplied from public funds rather than from private resources.", :sources ["Shekalim 4:1" "Leviticus 2:13"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 3, :occurrences ["מלח"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "